RS-232 AND MODBUS ASCII COMMUNICATION PROJECT
=============================================
Author: Szymon Wilczek


PROJECT OVERVIEW
=============================
This project implements a serial communication system between two DTE (Data
Terminal Equipment) stations. It focuses on the RS-232 standard and the
MODBUS ASCII protocol, providing a cross-platform Hardware Abstraction Layer
(HAL) for Linux and Windows, a custom protocol engine, and a multi-threaded
Text User Interface (TUI).

The project was developed as part of the IWSK course at the Silesian University
of Technology (PolSL).


ARCHITECTURE
=============================
The software is divided into three distinct layers to ensure modularity and
ease of hardware migration:

* HAL (Hardware Abstraction Layer): Isolates OS-specific serial port handling
  (termios for Linux, WinAPI for Windows). It provides a unified interface
  for I/O operations and modem line control.
* Protocol Engine (MODBUS/RS-232): A hardware-agnostic layer that handles
  data framing, checksum (LRC) calculation, and ASCII encoding/decoding.
* Presentation Layer (TUI): A multi-threaded CLI application. The background
  thread handles asynchronous RX polling, while the main thread manages user
  input and UI rendering via ANSI escape codes.


HARDWARE & EMULATION SETUP
=============================
The system operates at an industry-standard 9600 bps (8N1). This ensures
maximum noise immunity and stability in physical environments. The frame
terminator strictly follows the MODBUS ASCII specification: CR LF (\r\n).

Emulation (Software-only)
-------------------------
To test the logic without physical hardware, virtual serial ports are used:

  Linux: Use 'socat' to create a virtual null-modem pair:
  $ socat -d -d PTY,link=/tmp/ttyV0,raw,echo=0 PTY,link=/tmp/ttyV1,raw,echo=0

  Windows: Use 'com0com' to create a virtual COM port pair.

Physical Connection
-------------------------
The system was tested using physical cross-platform communication:
* 2x USB-to-RS232 (DB9) adapters (UGREEN CR104 - PL2303).
* 1x Null Modem Cable (Female-to-Female) with full crossover for hardware 
  flow control.


FEATURES & TUI COMMANDS
=============================
The application provides an interactive menu covering the full assignment.

Link configuration:
  c  Set transmission parameters: baud (150..115200), data bits (7/8),
     parity (N/E/O), stop bits (1/2). Applied live via set_config.
  f  Flow control: None / XON-XOFF (software) / RTS-CTS / DTR-DSR (hardware).
  t  Terminator: None / CR / LF / CR-LF / custom 1-2 byte (entered as HEX).
  i  Show current configuration.

RS-232 transfer:
  1  Send text: payload + configured terminator (window "Nadawanie").
  2  Send binary: raw bytes entered in HEX (simple hex editor).
  3  Send binary file: open a file and stream its raw bytes.
  4  Transaction: send a query and await the reply within a timeout.
  5  PING: send probe, await PONG, report round-trip delay. The passive
     listener auto-answers an incoming PING with PONG.

Modem lines:
  d  Toggle DTR.   r  Toggle RTS.   l  Read DSR/CTS/DCD/RI status.

MODBUS station (menu 'm'):
  - Select role: Master or Slave.
  - Master: operator-defined frame (slave address, function, arguments),
    automatic LRC + CR-LF, addressed and broadcast (address 0) transactions,
    with timeout (0..10 s), retransmissions (0..5) and inter-character gap
    (0..1 s).
  - Slave: address 1..247, verifies the destination address, rejects frames
    with a bad LRC, replies with a normal or exception (Modbus error) response,
    and enforces the inter-character gap during reception.
  - Application commands: function 1 writes text Master->Slave ("Tekst
    odebrany" on the Slave); function 2 reads text Slave->Master.
  - Both stations print every sent and received frame as a HEX dump.

NOTE on flow control: None, XON/XOFF (software) and RTS/CTS (hardware,
termios CRTSCTS) are fully supported and verified on the PL2303 adapters.
Automatic DTR/DSR flow control is not offered by termios, so it is reported
as unsupported; the DTR line is still controllable manually (commands d/l).


VERIFICATION & TEST SCENARIOS
=============================
Scenario 1: Loopback & Protocol Integrity
Goal: Verify that the Modbus engine correctly encodes and decodes frames.
1. Connect Station A (Linux) and Station B (Windows) via physical cable.
2. Send a Modbus frame (Option 2) from Station A.
3. Expectation: Station B buffers fragmented physical UART bytes, detects
   the \n terminator, validates the LRC, and displays [MODBUS VALID].

Scenario 2: Error Handling (Corrupted Frames)
Goal: Ensure the system rejects invalid protocol data.
1. From Station A, send a raw text string (Option 1) formatted as a Modbus 
   frame but with an intentional error in the LRC (e.g., :01014849FF\r\n).
2. Expectation: Station B receives the data but labels it as [RAW TEXT] 
   instead of [MODBUS VALID]. The decoder correctly returns -EBADMSG after 
   failing the checksum verification.

Scenario 3: Hardware Flow Control (Handshake) & OS Quirks
Goal: Verify manual control of modem lines using physical adapters.
1. Connect two PCs via physical adapters and a Null Modem cable.
2. On Station A, toggle DTR (Option 3) and RTS (Option 4).
3. On Station B, read modem status (Option 5).
4. Expectations & Hardware Quirks:
   - Toggling RTS on sender correctly changes CTS on receiver.
   - Toggling DTR on sender changes DCD (Data Carrier Detect) instead of 
     DSR on receiver, due to professional Null-Modem cable crossover.
   - On Windows, reading local modem status (GetCommModemStatus) only 
     returns input pins. Local output pins (DTR/RTS) will read as 0.


BUILD INSTRUCTIONS
=============================
The project uses CMake for cross-platform builds. The setup includes a 
custom target to automatically cross-compile the Windows .exe using MinGW.

  $ mkdir build && cd build
  $ cmake ..
  $ make
  $ ./polsl_iwsk_app_linux /dev/ttyUSB0


